				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-430723ff elementor-widget elementor-widget-theme-post-content" data-id="430723ff" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="theme-post-content.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<div class="tasty-recipes-quick-links"><a class="tasty-recipes-jump-link tasty-recipes-scrollto" href="#tasty-recipes-82802-jump-target">Jump to Recipe</a><span>&middot;</span><a class="tasty-recipes-print-link" href="https://www.amysnutritionkitchen.com/easy-tofu-lettuce-wraps-with-mushrooms/print/82802/" target="_blank">Print Recipe</a></div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This recipe is part of my ongoing tofu series, where I&#8217;m sharing different ways to prepare and enjoy tofu. You&#8217;ve heard me say this before, but I really believe it&#8217;s important to try foods prepared in different ways to find the version that works for <strong>you</strong>. That doesn&#8217;t mean forcing yourself to eat foods you don&#8217;t like. It means being open to experimenting and giving yourself the opportunity to have a good experience with a food.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Think about it: sometimes we try a food that has been prepared in a way that just doesn&#8217;t appeal to us, and we decide we don&#8217;t like that food. But when you try it prepared differently—with the right texture, seasonings, and flavors—it can be a completely different experience and may even change your opinion. Tofu is a perfect example. I&#8217;ve heard from plenty of people who have tried tofu and decided it&#8217;s &#8220;not for them,&#8221; but tofu itself has a very mild flavor and takes on the flavors of whatever you pair it with. That&#8217;s why the sauce, seasonings, and how you prepare it can make such a difference.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These lettuce wraps are savory, slightly sweet, a little spicy, and packed with umami from the mushrooms, soy sauce, sesame oil, and hoisin. And because they&#8217;re served in crisp butter lettuce leaves, you get that great contrast between the warm filling and cool, crunchy lettuce.</p>



