<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Ingredients: Tofu Lettuce Wraps</strong></h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Tofu</strong>: <strong>package extra-firm tofu</strong>: Tofu is the protein base for these lettuce wraps. For this recipe, you&#8217;re going to grate the tofu rather than cube it, which creates small pieces that resemble crumbled ground meat and gives the tofu plenty of surface area to absorb the sauce. Press the tofu first to remove excess water. This helps the tofu brown in the skillet rather than simply steaming, and it also gives the finished filling a better texture.</li>



<li><strong>Baby Bella Mushrooms</strong>: Mushrooms add a meaty texture and savory, earthy flavor to the filling. Finely chopping them allows them to blend in with the grated tofu while adding another layer of umami.</li>



<li><strong>Sesame Oil</strong>: adds a rich, nutty flavor that works especially well with the soy sauce, hoisin, garlic, and mushrooms. It helps bring all of those savory flavors together while adding another layer of depth to the filling.</li>



<li><strong>Garlic</strong>: adds another layer of savory flavor and works especially well with the soy sauce, hoisin, and mushrooms.</li>



<li><strong>Soy Sauce</strong>: brings saltiness and umami to the filling. It also helps season the tofu and mushrooms as they cook.  Use the reduced-sodium version here, as even it is relatively high in sodium.</li>



<li><strong>Rice Wine Vinegar</strong>: A little acidity helps balance the salty and sweet flavors in the dish. Rice wine vinegar adds brightness without overpowering the other ingredients.</li>



<li><strong>Hoisin Sauce</strong>: is where a lot of the flavor comes together. It&#8217;s slightly sweet, savory, and rich, and it coats the tofu and mushrooms beautifully. Many varieties can be high in both added sugar and sodium, but there are more options available than ever. Check the labels and shop around to find one that works best for you!</li>



<li><strong>Sriracha</strong>: adds a little heat to the filling. One teaspoon gives the dish some spice without making it overwhelmingly hot, but you can adjust it to your preference and add up to a tablespoon!</li>



<li><strong>Butter Lettuce</strong>: makes the perfect wrap because the leaves are tender enough to fold around the filling while still providing a fresh, crisp contrast to the warm tofu and mushroom mixture.</li>
</ul>



